    "q-77616": {
      "dom_id": "step-q77616",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14187003/assets/dc12a180a283b34a36d1f44d6ea197b017138632f1d1918255a1b4253b541fd0.png)\n\nConsider the triangle $△ABC$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{CH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ If $m∠BAC={120}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠HCA.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HAC+m∠BAC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC+{120}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HAC & ={60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△AHC$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HCA+m∠HAC+m∠AHC & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{60}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA+{150}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HCA & ={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
